Violet square-shaped crystals of Ti-(Ti·TBP) were synthesized through a solvothermal reaction between \(\mathrm{TiCl}_{4}{\cdot}2\mathrm{THF}\) and 5,10,15,20-tetra(p-benzoato)porphyrin (H4TBP) in N,N-dimethylformamide with acetic acid (AcOH) as the modulator at \(120\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 7 days. Single crystal X-ray diffraction studies revealed that the TBP ligands were metalated with Ti during crystal growth and the Ti-coordinated TBP (Ti·TBP) ligands were linked by infinite Ti-oxo chain SBUs to form a 3D framework of the new topology with a point symbol of \(\{4^{18}.6^{2}.8^{4}.10\}\{4^{2}.6\}_{2}\{4^{6}.6^{9}\}_{2}\{4\}_{2}\) (Figure 1a and S2-3, SI). Each repeat unit of the Ti-oxo chain has
five \(\mathrm{Ti^{4+}}\) ions that are bridged by carboxylate groups from TBP or acetate ligands and terminated by hydroxide groups (Figure 2a and S1, SI). Negligible Cl was detected in Ti-(Ti·TBP) by X-ray fluorescence \(\mathrm{(Cl:Ti~=~0.0016:1)}\), leading to a formula of \(\mathrm{[Ti_{5}(T i\cdot T B P)_{2}(O A c)_{2}(O H)_{4}](O A c)_{8}}\).
Lowering the reaction temperature to \(80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) led to the synthesis of Ti-TBP nMOF with non-metalated TBP ligands of the composition \(\mathrm{[Ti_{5}(T B P)_{2}(O A c)_{2}(O H)_{6}](O A c)_{4}}\). UV-Vis spectrum of Ti-TBP showed four characteristic Q-bands for non-metalated TBP ligands (Figure S5, SI). Thermogravimetric analysis of Ti-TBP showed a weight loss of \(82.9\%\) in the 300 to \(600\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) range, matching the expected value of \(82.4\%\) (Figure S6, SI). By combining inductively coupled plasma-mass spectrometry (ICPMS) analysis of Ti and UV-Vis analysis of TBP in digested Ti-TBP, we determined the Ti:TBP ratio as \(2.67\pm0.16\), which is close to 2.5 expected for Ti-TBP but much lower than 3.5 expected for Ti(Ti·TBP).
Transmission electron microscope (TEM) imaging of Ti-TBP revealed square nanoplates with a diameter of \(\sim150\;\mathrm{nm}\) (Figure 2a) while atomic force microscopy (AFM) topography of Ti-TBP gave a plate thickness of \(\sim20\;\mathrm{nm}\) (Figure 2c and S4, SI). Dynamic light scattering (DLS) measurements gave a diameter of \(100.1\pm4.0\;\mathrm{nm}\) for Ti-TBP (Figure 2e). The porous structure of Ti-TBP was confirmed by nitrogen sorption isotherms at \(77\,\mathrm{K}\) with a Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area of \(527.7\;\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\) (Figure 2d). High
resolution TEM (HRTEM) imaging and fast Fourier transform (FFT) patterns of Ti-TBP revealed a 4-fold symmetry, consistent with the Ti-TBP structure projected in the (010) direction (Figure 2b). The distance between two adjacent lattice points in HRTEM was measured to be \(\sim1.6\;\mathrm{nm}\), matching the distance between the centers of two adjacent Ti-chain SBUs. Moreover, powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) pattern of Ti-TBP matched well with that simulated from its idealized structure (Figure 2f).
To demonstrate that Ti-TBP-enabled type I PDT can tolerate hypoxia of solid tumors, we mimicked hypoxic cancer cell environments with an oxygen-free aqueous solution containing either \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) (in high concentration in hypoxic cancer cells)\(^{22}\) or glutathione (GSH, a ubiquitous antioxidant in cells).\(^{28}\) Upon light irradiation, Ti-TBP effectively reduced \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) through \(\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}\) to generate highly cytotoxic \(\mathrm{OH}\), while Hf-TBP and \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{TBP}\) or Ti
TBP without light irradiation did not enhance \(\cdot\mathrm{OH}\) generation (Figure 3e and S8, SI). Furthermore, \(\mathrm{TBP^{+}}\) elicited oxidative stress by oxidizing GSH to glutathione disulfide (GSSG) as determined by high performance liquid chromatograph (Figure 3f and S9, SI).
