The cytotoxicity of Ti-TBP-mediated PDT was investigated in vitro in CT26 cells. ICP-MS analysis demonstrated efficient uptake
of Ti-TBP and Hf-TBP by CT26 cells (Figure S17, SI). Although \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{TBP}\), Hf-TBP, and Ti-TBP can all generate \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}\)was only detected in cells treated with Ti-TBP (Figure 5a and S18, SI). The in vitro generation of \(\mathrm{OH}\) was verified by direct \(\mathrm{OH}\) detection via coumarin-3-carboxylic acid assay (Figure 5b and S19, SI) and by DNA double strand break (DSB) quantification with \(\gamma\)-H2AX assay (Figure 5c and S20, SI), both of which showed that \(\mathrm{OH}\) was only detected in Ti-TBP treated cells. Confocal imaging and flow cytometry using an Annexin V/dead cell apoptosis kit showed that significant numbers of cells underwent apoptosis when treated with Ti-TBP (Figure 5d and S21-22, SI). MTS assay and live/dead cell confocal microscopic images showed that Ti-TBP outperformed Hf-TBP with \(\mathrm{IC}_{50}\) values of \(3.4 \pm 0.7\) and \(7.8 \pm 2.4~\upmu\mathrm{M}\), respectively (Figure 5e-f, and S23-24, SI).
The therapeutic effects of Ti-TBP-mediated PDT were next evaluated in vivo on a colorectal adenocarcinoma model of CT26-tumor bearing BALB/c mice. When the tumors reached 100-150 \(\mathrm{mm}^{3}\), Ti-TBP, Hf-TBP, \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{TBP}\) or PBS was injected intratumorally at a TBP doses of \(0.2~\upmu\mathrm{mol}\) following by light irradiation \((650\;\mathrm{nm}, 180\;\mathrm{J}/\mathrm{cm}^{2})\). Ti-TBP treatment led to effective tumor regression of \(98.4\%\) in volume with a cure rate of \(60\%\) (3 out of 5), when compared to that of the PBS dark control on Day 20. Hf-TBP and \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{TBP}\) treatment showed moderate and slight tumor inhibition, respectively (Figure 5g). The averaged weights of excised tumors on Day 20 treated with Ti-TBP, Hf-TBP, \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{TBP}\) or PBS were \(0.027 \pm 0.037\;\mathrm{g}, 0.127 \pm 0.03\;\mathrm{g}, 0.617 \pm 0.168\;\mathrm{g}, \mathrm{or} 1.734 \pm 0.291\;\mathrm{g}\), respectively (Figure S26-27, SI). H&E staining indicated severe necrosis of tumor slices from Ti-TBP treatment (Figure S28, SI). Steady body weights, similar weight gain patterns, and no difference in behaviors and organ functions were observed in all groups, indicating lack of systemic toxicity for Ti-TBP treatment (Figure S25, SI). The lack of abnormalities on histological images of frozen major organ slices further supported the non-toxic nature of Ti-TBP-mediated PDT treatment (Figure S29, SI).
In summary, we report the synthesis of Ti-TBP and its use in hypoxia-tolerant type IPDT with superb anti-cancer efficacy. Upon light irradiation, the proximity of Ti-oxo chain SBUs to TBP ligands \((\sim\!1.1\;\mathrm{nm})\) facilitates ET to generate \(\mathrm{TBP^{++}}\) and \(\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}\), propagating the generation of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}\), \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\), and \(\mathrm{OH}\). Our work uncovers a new strategy to implement and understand type I PDT using nMOFs.
